    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d207">

                <pb facs="0292" n="199" sameAs="frus:frus1935v04/pg199"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">721.23/2420: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Colombia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Washington</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-01T11:00:00-05:00">January 1, 1935—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1. Colombian Senate failed to approve Rio pact<anchor n="2" xml:id="d207-f1"/> so
                    that ratifications could be exchanged on December 31st. It has been the general
                    feeling that approval would have been granted if a vote had been taken but this
                    was prevented by the parliamentary tactics of the Conservative opposition.
                    Conservative leader Laureano Gomez’s term as President of the Senate expired on
                    the 31st. Public sentiment is apparently in favor of the pact and it may be
                    ratified early in January under a new Senate President. It is understood that
                    the Government is considering trying to obtain Peru’s consent to a later
                    exchange of ratifications.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
